// U3 L2 Energy Intake and Expenditure â€” 30 questions

::U3L2-01::A food Calorie generally means a {=kilocalorie ~gram ~vitamin ~serving}

::U3L2-02::Energy intake is {=energy obtained from foods and beverages ~only exercise energy ~a weight target ~activity intensity}

::U3L2-03::Energy expenditure includes {=basic functions, activity, and other physiological processes ~activity only ~food only ~body weight only}

::U3L2-04::Basal energy needs support {=essential body functions at rest ~vigorous activity only ~protein digestion only ~body-size measurement}

::U3L2-05::Metabolism refers to {=chemical processes that sustain life and use energy ~exercise duration ~one universal energy value ~body appearance}

::U3L2-06::Energy balance describes {=the relationship between intake and expenditure over time ~a command to minimize intake ~activity only ~appearance}

::U3L2-07::An estimated value is {=an informed approximation ~always exact ~a diagnosis ~a prescription}

::U3L2-08::Which variable may influence an activity estimate? {=Intensity ~Page color ~Question number ~Course title}

::U3L2-09::A serving is a standardized listed amount; a portion is {=the amount selected or consumed ~always identical to a serving ~an intensity unit ~body size}

::U3L2-10::Why is activity only one part of expenditure? {=The body also uses energy for basic and other physiological functions. ~The body uses no energy at rest. ~Only exercise uses energy. ~Intake equals expenditure.}

::U3L2-11::What is 420 + 610 + 230 + 740? {=2000 ~1900 ~2100 ~1800}

::U3L2-12::What is 120 + 210 estimated Calories? {=330 ~320 ~430 ~90}

::U3L2-13::What is 190 + 220 + 150 estimated Calories? {=560 ~460 ~570 ~340}

::U3L2-14::Walking for 40 minutes has a higher provided estimate than walking for 20 minutes at the same stated intensity. What is supported? {=Duration and the provided estimate are higher. ~Intensity is necessarily higher. ~Health is proven. ~The person should eat less.}

::U3L2-15::If both activities are moderate but one lasts longer, what clearly changed? {=Duration ~Intensity ~Health status ~Body size}

::U3L2-16::A 20-minute vigorous circuit has a higher estimate than a 45-minute light walk. What is supported? {=The circuit has the higher supplied estimate despite shorter duration. ~Longer means more intense. ~The circuit guarantees health. ~The walk was harmful.}

::U3L2-17::Why label wearable calorie values as estimates? {=Actual expenditure varies with physiology, efficiency, body size, and method. ~Wearables use no data. ~Everyone expends the same energy. ~They measure total health.}

::U3L2-18::One fictional intake table can support {=the sum of its supplied values ~long-term health status ~ideal body weight ~a diagnosis}

::U3L2-19::Which is unsupported by one day of calorie data? {=This person is healthier than another. ~One total is greater. ~One activity lasted longer. ~Expenditure values are estimates.}

::U3L2-20::Why can correct arithmetic still produce a weak health conclusion? {=The dataset may omit important context. ~Arithmetic never matters. ~Calories determine all health. ~Estimates are exact.}

::U3L2-21::Which is a relevant limitation? {=The table may omit portions, preparation, physiology, and measurement error. ~It contains numbers. ~Activities have names. ~The student is fictional.}

::U3L2-22::â€œCalories are unhealthyâ€ is inaccurate because {=calories are energy units and health interpretation requires context ~calories must always be minimized ~only exercise has calories ~calories measure body size}

::U3L2-23::Which statement best corrects â€œexercise burns off foodâ€? {=Activity has many health and functional purposes and is not punishment for eating. ~Food must be earned. ~The statement is always correct. ~Activity has no health purpose.}

::U3L2-24::A fictional student has prolonged activity, little fluid, and fatigue. What is strongest? {=Fueling and hydration may be relevant, but the data do not support diagnosis or an exact prescription. ~The student must lose weight. ~The estimate proves illness. ~Only intensity matters.}

::U3L2-25::Why can one meal or session not determine overall balance? {=Energy relationships and health develop across time and broader context. ~Single events contain no data. ~Only weight matters. ~Estimates are diagnoses.}

::U3L2-26::Energy availability should be understood as {=energy remaining to support body functions after activity demands ~the lowest possible intake ~appearance ~a one-table diagnosis}

::U3L2-27::Day A intake is 1970 and Day B is 1740 supplied Calories. What is supported? {=Day A is 230 Calories higher. ~Day A is healthier. ~Day B should restrict food. ~The difference predicts weight change.}

::U3L2-28::Day A activity estimate is 285 and Day B is 510 Calories. What is the difference? {=225 estimated Calories ~205 ~795 ~230 exact Calories}

::U3L2-29::Critical analysis requires students to {=calculate, interpret, and state limitations ~judge health from one number ~treat estimates as exact ~recommend restriction}

::U3L2-30::Calories alone cannot measure overall health because {=health also includes adequacy, activity, sleep, mental and social wellness, safety, and other factors ~calories are never useful ~appearance is enough ~expenditure is the only factor}

